  "query_preview": "/Monday through Friday/...\nIssued 228 PM MDT THU OCT 8 2026\nCool and unsettled conditions will continue into Sunday. As the low \nthat will bring us our cool down digs south into the Great Basin, \nmoisture from Rachel will be transported north in the southerly flow\naloft. While the bulk of this remnant moisture should remain to our\neast and south, there is still some uncertainty in the westward\nextent of it. How far into our area it gets will influence \nprecipitation totals generally from Mountain Home on east. Right now,\nthe forecast carries around a half inch of precip over the western\nMagic Valley and Boise Mountains, tapering off as you move west to\naround 0.05 inches at Boise from Sunday 6am MDT to Monday 6am MDT.\nHowever, these totals will be higher should the plume of remnant \nmoisture set up a little farther west. The latest NBM 75th percentile\nQPF over that period gets to around an inch in the western Magic\nValley, which would be a reasonable higher end total. The potential\nfor significant rainfall will continue to be monitored in the days\nto come. The southerly flow aloft will keep snow levels on the higher\nside across SW-Idaho this weekend, with snow levels ranging from \n7-9 kft MSL. Temperatures on Sunday will drop to 5-10 degrees below\nnormal, with localized frost/freeze conditions Sunday morning \nbefore more widespread frost/freeze conditions Monday morning. \nTo put it simply, expect cool, cloudy, and rainy conditions Sunday\ninto Monday across SW Idaho.\n\nAs the low moves east through Tuesday, uncertainty remains on how \nfar north/south it will track and whether or not our area will get \ninto the wrap-around precip associated with it. For now, we are\nmaintaining the 20-40% chance of precipitation come Tuesday, \nhighest in southern and central Idaho. Come Wednesday, the \ndynamic pattern looks to continue, with ensembles coming into\nagreement on a shortwave trough moving across the Northern Rockies\nbefore ridging sets in off the coast come Thursday. The shortwave\ntrough will aid in maintaining cooler than normal temperatures \nthrough mid week, with precipitation favored over the Central\nIdaho Mountains. With light snow accumulations possible above\n6-7 kft MSL.",
